            <div xml:id="d680">

                <pb facs="0647" n="635" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg635"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">340.1115A/326: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-12-08T18:00:00+03:00">December 8, 1939—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 8—2:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1048. Department’s telegram number 251, November 28, 7 p.m. In reply to its
                    repeated inquiries the Embassy was informed orally this morning by the Peoples
                    Commissariat for Foreign Affairs that funds may be sent to persons either of
                    Soviet or foreign nationality <pb facs="0648" n="636" xml:id="pg636"/> in
                    Soviet-occupied Poland provided such funds are remitted to the payee through the
                    medium of the Bank for Foreign Trade of the U. S. S. R. which bank will have
                    payment effected through the appropriate local branch of the State Bank of the
                    U. S. S. R. Such remittances will be paid to the payee in Soviet currency at the
                    official rate of exchange. Inasmuch as Soviet currency may not be taken out of
                    the Soviet Union the official making the foregoing statement was requested to
                    make some arrangement whereby American citizens receiving remittances for living
                    and travel expenses may be permitted to receive funds in dollars or otherwise so
                    that they may be in possession of needed funds beyond the Soviet frontier. He
                    stated that he would present the Embassy’s request to the competent
                    authorities.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
